```js
const EMBEDDED_CSV = `district,year,children_due,children_fully_immunised,source
Gaya,2022,1000,780,synthetic smoke fixture
...`;

function parseCSV(text) {
  const lines = text.split(/\r?\n/).map(l => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  if (!lines.length) return [];
  const header = lines[0].split(',').map(h => h.trim());
  return lines.slice(1).map(line => {
    const cells = line.split(',');
    const row = {};
    header.forEach((h, i) => row[h] = (cells[i] ?? '').trim());
    return row;
  });
}

function toNum(v) {
  const n = parseFloat(String(v).replace(/,/g, ''));
  return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : 0;
}

let ROWS = [];
async function loadData() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('district_immunisation.csv', { cache: 'no-store' });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.status);
    ROWS = parseCSV(await res.text());
  } catch {
    ROWS = parseCSV(EMBEDDED_CSV);
  }
}

function fmt(n) { return n.toLocaleString('en-IN'); }
```

Hmm, en-IN or en-US? The districts are in Bihar state, India (Gaya, Nalanda, Purnia). en-IN locale grouping (lakh/crore style: 1,100 is the same up to 100k). For numbers under 100k it's identical. For safety, I'll use 'en-US'... actually, for an Indian context en-IN is nice, but the number formatting is identical below 100,000. Let me keep en-US for neutrality. No wait, the data is about Indian districts — I'll use en-IN. Either is fine. Let me go with en-IN.

